Runtime: Execution

Purpose:
  Define the execution engine and state machine for task execution
  in the SDDRA system. Execution is the runtime layer where tasks
  are actually performed, state is managed, and results are validated.

Owns: ./

Scope:
  Execution vs Task distinction
  Execution ID and lifecycle
  Execution state machine
  State transitions and rules
  Event-driven execution
  Execution events and immutability
  Action IDs and tool calls
  Execution traces
  Checkpoints and resume
  Durable execution
  Crash recovery
  Unknown state handling
  Retry policies and backoff
  Timeouts and cancellation
  Compensation and rollback
  Idempotency and idempotency keys
  Execution locks and leases
  Heartbeats and stuck execution detection
  Agent runtime flow
  Action proposals
  Deterministic boundary
  Completion criteria and validation
  Execution artifacts and provenance
  Execution budget

Principles:
  Task is specification; Execution is actual run.
  LLM proposes actions; Execution Engine manages state.
  State machine is deterministic; LLM does not change state directly.
  Events are immutable — history is append-only.
  Unknown result is NOT failure.
  Execution state persists in DB, not RAM.

Execution:
  definition:
    actual runtime run of a task with full state management

  relationship_to_task:
    one task can have multiple executions
    example:
      TASK-100 -> EXEC-1 (failed)
      TASK-100 -> EXEC-2 (retry)
      TASK-100 -> EXEC-3 (success)

  structure:
    execution_id: string (unique, e.g. EXEC-2026-00125)
    task_id: string
    state: string
    current_action: string
    checkpoint: string
    worker_id: string
    lease: string
    context_snapshot: string
    policy_snapshot: string
    retry_count: integer
    budget:
      tokens: integer
      cost: float
      duration: string
      tool_calls: integer

ExecutionID:
  rule: every execution has unique ID
  rule: execution ID is used in all events
  rule: execution ID never changes

ExecutionLifecycle:
  PENDING -> READY -> RUNNING -> VALIDATING -> COMPLETED
  RUNNING -> WAITING_APPROVAL -> RUNNING
  RUNNING -> WAITING_INPUT -> RUNNING
  RUNNING -> PAUSED -> RUNNING
  RUNNING -> FAILED -> RETRYING -> RUNNING
  RUNNING -> CANCELLED
  RUNNING -> BLOCKED

ExecutionStates:
  PENDING:
    description: execution created but not ready
    transitions: -> READY

  READY:
    description: all prerequisites met
    prerequisites:
      - context resolved
      - agent assigned
      - skill resolved
      - policy evaluated
      - dependencies resolved
    transitions: -> RUNNING

  RUNNING:
    description: agent is actively working
    transitions:
      -> WAITING_APPROVAL
      -> WAITING_INPUT
      -> PAUSED
      -> VALIDATING
      -> FAILED
      -> CANCELLED
      -> BLOCKED

  PAUSED:
    description: execution temporarily suspended
    transitions: -> RUNNING

  WAITING_APPROVAL:
    description: waiting for human approval
    transitions:
      -> RUNNING (approved)
      -> CANCELLED (rejected)

  WAITING_INPUT:
    description: waiting for user input
    example: "Which payment provider should be used?"
    transitions: -> RUNNING

  VALIDATING:
    description: validating acceptance criteria
    transitions:
      -> COMPLETED (pass)
      -> FAILED (fail)

  COMPLETED:
    description: acceptance criteria met
    transitions: none (terminal)

  FAILED:
    description: execution failed
    transitions:
      -> RETRYING
      -> ESCALATED

  CANCELLED:
    description: execution cancelled by user or orchestrator
    transitions: none (terminal)

  BLOCKED:
    description: execution blocked by dependency or external factor
    transitions: -> RUNNING (when unblocked)

StateTransitionRules:
  allowed_transitions:
    PENDING -> READY
    READY -> RUNNING
    RUNNING -> WAITING_APPROVAL
    RUNNING -> WAITING_INPUT
    RUNNING -> PAUSED
    RUNNING -> VALIDATING
    RUNNING -> FAILED
    RUNNING -> CANCELLED
    RUNNING -> BLOCKED
    VALIDATING -> COMPLETED
    VALIDATING -> FAILED
    WAITING_APPROVAL -> RUNNING
    WAITING_APPROVAL -> CANCELLED
    PAUSED -> RUNNING
    FAILED -> RETRYING
    RETRYING -> RUNNING
    BLOCKED -> RUNNING

  forbidden_transitions:
    COMPLETED -> RUNNING
    CANCELLED -> RUNNING

EventDrivenExecution:
  events:
    - ExecutionCreated
    - AgentAssigned
    - ContextResolved
    - ExecutionStarted
    - ToolCalled
    - ToolCompleted
    - ToolFailed
    - ApprovalRequested
    - ApprovalGranted
    - ApprovalRejected
    - ValidationStarted
    - ExecutionCompleted
    - ExecutionFailed
    - ExecutionCancelled
    - ExecutionPaused
    - ExecutionResumed
    - CheckpointCreated

EventLog:
  example:
    EXEC-500
      1. ExecutionCreated
      2. AgentAssigned
      3. ContextResolved
      4. ExecutionStarted
      5. ToolCalled
      6. ToolCompleted
      7. ValidationStarted
      8. ExecutionCompleted

  rule: events are immutable
  rule: events are append-only
  rule: events MUST be in chronological order

StateDerivedFromEvents:
  principle:
    current state can be reconstructed from event log

  example:
    ExecutionCreated + ExecutionStarted + ToolCompleted -> RUNNING

EventSourcing:
  definition:
    storing state changes as events rather than current state

  practical_model:
    Current State + Immutable Event Log

  rule: event log is source of truth for history
  rule: current state is optimized for queries

EventImmutability:
  rule: events cannot be modified after creation
  rule: corrections create new events, not modifications
  example:
    TOOL_FAILED
    TOOL_RETRIED
    TOOL_COMPLETED

Action:
  definition:
    discrete unit of work within execution

  structure:
    action_id: string (ACTION-001)
    execution_id: string
    type: string (READ, WRITE, TEST, DEPLOY, etc.)
    tool: string
    parameters: object
    idempotency_key: string

ActionID:
  rule: every action has unique ID within execution
  rule: action ID is used in events and traces

ToolCall:
  flow:
    Action -> Tool Call -> Tool Result -> Event

  structure:
    tool_call_id: string
    action_id: string
    tool: string
    arguments: object
    result: object
    status: success | failure | unknown
    duration: timestamp

ExecutionTrace:
  structure:
    EXEC-500
      ├── ACTION-001
      │     └── read(file)
      ├── ACTION-002
      │     └── write(file)
      ├── ACTION-003
      │     └── test()
      └── ACTION-004
            └── git.diff()

  rule: every execution has complete trace

Idempotency:
  definition:
    operation can be retried without side effects

  problem:
    network timeout -> agent doesn't know if action completed
    retry without idempotency -> duplicate results

  solution:
    idempotency_key: EXEC-500-ACTION-17

  rule: tool calls MUST support idempotency keys
  rule: retry uses same idempotency key

Checkpoint:
  definition:
    saved state that can be resumed from

  example:
    Checkpoint 1: Architecture ready
    Checkpoint 2: Implementation ready
    Checkpoint 3: Tests passed

  rule: checkpoints are created before risky operations
  rule: checkpoints are immutable once created

Resume:
  flow:
    Crash -> Restart -> Load Checkpoint -> Restore Context -> Resume

  rule: execution can resume from last checkpoint
  rule: context is restored from snapshot

DurableExecution:
  principle:
    execution state lives in persistent storage, not RAM

  rule: execution state survives process crash
  rule: execution state survives server restart

CrashRecovery:
  flow:
    Find interrupted executions
      -> Inspect last known action
      -> Check tool result
      -> Resume / Retry / Fail

ToolResultUncertainty:
  principle:
    unknown result is NOT failure

  states:
    - SUCCESS: tool completed successfully
    - FAILURE: tool failed definitively
    - UNKNOWN: result is uncertain (timeout, network issue)

  rule: UNKNOWN requires investigation, not blind retry

RetryPolicy:
  retry_types:
    IMMEDIATE: retry immediately
    FIXED_DELAY: retry after fixed delay
    EXPONENTIAL_BACKOFF: retry with increasing delay
    MANUAL: requires human intervention
    NO_RETRY: do not retry

  exponential_backoff:
    retry 1 -> 1 sec
    retry 2 -> 2 sec
    retry 3 -> 4 sec
    retry 4 -> 8 sec

  rule: retry is only for transient failures
  rule: non-retryable failures do not retry

RetryLimit:
  rule: max_retries = 3 (configurable per task)
  rule: after exhaustion -> FAILED or ESCALATED

Timeout:
  types:
    - Task timeout
    - Action timeout
    - Tool timeout
    - Approval timeout

  example:
    tool_timeout = 30 sec

  rule: timeouts are configurable per task type

Cancellation:
  flow:
    RUNNING -> CANCELLING -> CANCELLED

  rule: cancellation is graceful
  rule: running tools are interrupted

Compensation:
  definition:
    compensating action for irreversible previous action

  example:
    Create Order -> Reserve Inventory -> Charge Payment -> Create Shipment
    Shipment failed -> Release Inventory -> Refund Payment -> Cancel Order

  rule: compensation is used when rollback is impossible

Rollback:
  definition:
    return to previous state

  vs_compensation:
    Rollback: return to previous state
    Compensation: counter-action for irreversible action

  rule: rollback is preferred when possible
  rule: compensation is used for distributed systems

Saga:
  definition:
    sequence of actions with compensating actions for each

  example:
    Create Order -> Reserve Inventory -> Charge Payment -> Create Shipment
    Compensation: Release Inventory -> Refund Payment -> Cancel Order

ExecutionTransaction:
  principle:
    entire AI workflow cannot be in single DB transaction

  reason:
    - LLM calls are long
    - Tools touch external systems

  practical_model:
    short DB transactions + durable execution state + compensation

ExecutionLock:
  rule: two workers cannot take same execution
  solution: distributed lock or DB-based claiming

Lease:
  structure:
    execution: EXEC-500
    worker: WORKER-01
    lease_until: 12:05

  rule: worker must renew lease before expiration
  rule: expired lease allows recovery by another worker

Heartbeat:
  flow:
    Worker -> heartbeat -> heartbeat -> heartbeat

  rule: long executions MUST send heartbeats
  rule: heartbeat interval is configurable

StuckExecution:
  detection:
    state = RUNNING
    last_heartbeat = 30 min ago

  rule: stuck execution triggers recovery

ExecutionOwnership:
  structure:
    execution:
      worker_id: WORKER-01
      agent_id: AGENT-CODER-01
      lease: 12:05

  rule: ownership is always known

AgentRuntime:
  definition:
    intelligence layer that proposes actions

  separation:
    Agent = intelligence
    Execution Engine = orchestration/state

  flow:
    Execution Engine -> Agent Runtime -> LLM -> Decision -> Action Proposal -> Policy Engine -> Tool

AgentActionProposal:
  structure:
    action:
      type: WRITE_FILE
      resource: auth/login.go
      operation: modify

  rule: proposal is NOT immediate execution
  rule: proposal goes through policy and validation

DeterministicBoundary:
  principle:
    LLM is non-deterministic; state machine, policy, permissions, transitions are deterministic

  rule: LLM does not change state directly
  rule: Execution Engine manages all state transitions

CompletionCriteria:
  rule: "Done" is not sufficient
  rule: acceptance criteria must be validated

  example:
    - Tests passed
    - API contract matches
    - No security violations
    - Required files updated

Validation:
  flow:
    EXECUTION -> VALIDATOR -> Acceptance Criteria -> PASS / FAIL

  result:
    PASS -> COMPLETED
    FAIL -> FAILED or RETRY

ExecutionArtifact:
  types:
    - code changes
    - documents
    - tests
    - migrations
    - logs
    - reports

  rule: artifacts are linked to execution

ArtifactProvenance:
  structure:
    artifact:
      id: ART-700
      execution: EXEC-500
      agent: AGENT-001
      action: ACTION-12
      source_revision: abc123

  rule: every artifact records its origin

ExecutionBudget:
  structure:
    budget:
      tokens: 200000
      cost: $2
      duration: 30m
      tool_calls: 100

  rule: budget is enforced during execution
  rule: budget exhaustion triggers PAUSE or DENY

ExecutionEngine:
  responsibilities:
    - start
    - pause
    - resume
    - cancel
    - retry
    - recover
    - checkpoint
    - transition

  rule: Execution Engine is deterministic
  rule: Execution Engine manages state, not LLM

ExecutionEngineArchitecture:
  TASK
    ↓
  EXECUTION ENGINE
    ↓
  ┌────────────┼────────────┐
  ▼            ▼            ▼
STATE MACHINE  EVENT LOG   CHECKPOINT
    ↓
  ACTION
    ↓
  POLICY ENGINE
    ↓
  TOOL
    ↓
  RESULT
    ↓
  VALIDATOR
    ↓
  NEXT STATE

Navigation:
  Runtime: @INDEX.sdd
  Context: @context.sdd
  Agent: @agent.sdd
  Session: @session.sdd
  Events: @events.sdd
  Orchestrator: @../orchestrator/INDEX.sdd
  Workflow: @../workflow/INDEX.sdd
  Tasks: @../tasks/INDEX.sdd

Rules:
  [RT-EXEC-01] Task is specification; Execution is actual run.
  [RT-EXEC-02] One task can have multiple executions.
  [RT-EXEC-03] Execution state machine is deterministic — LLM does not change state directly.
  [RT-EXEC-04] Events are immutable and append-only.
  [RT-EXEC-05] Unknown result is NOT failure — requires investigation.
  [RT-EXEC-06] Execution state persists in DB, not RAM.
  [RT-EXEC-07] Execution can resume from last checkpoint after crash.
  [RT-EXEC-08] Retry is only for transient failures; non-retryable failures do not retry.
  [RT-EXEC-09] Idempotency keys prevent duplicate effects on retry.
  [RT-EXEC-10] Checkpoints are created before risky operations.
  [RT-EXEC-11] Compensation is used when rollback is impossible.
  [RT-EXEC-12] Execution lock prevents two workers from taking same execution.
  [RT-EXEC-13] Lease and heartbeat detect stuck executions.
  [RT-EXEC-14] Agent proposes actions; Execution Engine validates and executes.
  [RT-EXEC-15] Completion requires validation, not just agent "Done".
  [RT-EXEC-16] Execution budget is enforced during execution.
  [RT-EXEC-17] Artifacts record provenance for traceability.
  [RT-EXEC-18] Agent MUST NOT add signatures or sign-offs to execution outputs.
  [RT-EXEC-19] Agent MUST NOT use emojis, icons, or decorative symbols in execution output headers or titles.

State: +
